{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '3930,3975p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md && sed -n '4068,4090p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md && nl -ba collab/1792223000000-architect-ts-php-r6.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md | sed -n '45,140p'\"","aggregated_output":"  cases pass.\n- JVM libraries, reflection, native methods, and concurrency are\n  explicitly supported or rejected.\n\n### Phase 3: PHP web and associative-array pilot\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- Mixed HTML/code modes.\n- Ordered-array semantics and key conversion.\n- Closure capture and reference behavior.\n- Request-context injection and stream-based output.\n- Module inclusion effects and supported type enforcement.\n\nExit criteria:\n\n- Mixed text and code preserve output order.\n- Arrays preserve ordering and alias/copy semantics.\n- Superglobals are reproducible from explicit request input.\n- Type declarations are not silently ignored.\n- Cross-request state leakage tests pass.\n\n### Phase 4: Language extension SDK and documentation (open SPI)\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- Package template, manifest validator, exporter helpers, lowering\n  examples, and prelude conventions.\n- Conformance runner and support-matrix generator.\n- Guides for dialects, parsing helpers, preprocessing, runtime\n  dependencies, and diagnostics.\n- License and regeneration tooling.\n- A documented external installation flow.\n\nExit criteria:\n\n- Add a fifth language package, such as a clearly bounded Go or Ruby\n  subset, outside Yang core.\n- Its implementation changes no core language dispatch, AST tags, or\n  evaluator cases.\n- At least one additional grammar can be installed in syntax-only mode\n  and honestly refuses unsupported executable lowering.\n- Two dialect packages coexist without registration collisions.\n\n### Phase 5: Multi-host verification and integration benchmarks\n\npeak memory, allocation, output amplification, cancellation latency, and\np50/p95/p99 caller-step latency.\n\n### 13.3 Completion criteria\n\nA pilot is complete only for its published support profile. The\nframework is architecturally complete when an independently packaged\nfifth frontend passes the same contracts and tri-host consumers can use\nit through local parsing, a configured service, or precompiled rows\nwithout changing Yang core.\n    45\t  int/float/bool    -> Error \"Cannot unset offset in a non-array variable\"\n    46\t  object            -> ArrayAccess::offsetUnset, else Error \"Cannot use object of type X as array\"\n    47\tUNSET_DIM(container, k): delete the bucket if present; DELREF its content; deps -= k; iterators updated (row 26)\n    48\tUNSET_OBJ(o, p):          the property slot: DELREF content; a typed property becomes uninitialized (A.4)\n    49\t```\n    50\t\n    51\t| # | Construct | Rule |\n    52\t|---|---|---|\n    53\t| 30 | `unset($x[k…])` | `FETCH_UNSET` to the container, then `UNSET_DIM`; `unset($a['missing']['child'])` on `[]` leaves `[]` unchanged |\n    54\t| 31 | `unset($o->p[k])`, `unset($o->p)` | property slot as container through `FETCH_OBJ_UNSET` (no separation, objects are handles); `__unset` when the property is inaccessible or absent |\n    55\t| 9 (revised) | round 5's row 9 | now defined as row 30; it no longer shares `FETCH_W` |\n    56\t\n    57\tFixtures: php-src `Zend/tests/unset_*.phpt`, `Zend/tests/bug*unset*.phpt`, `Zend/tests/isset_*` where paired; per-row programs for every non-array container kind and nested missing paths.\n    58\t\n    59\t### A.3 Temporary ownership (P1-3): `SEND_VAR`/`SEND_VAL`/`SEND_REF`, `RECV`, `RETURN`, `FREE`, live-temporary cleanup\n    60\t\n    61\tZend distinguishes **borrowed** operands (a CV or a fetched slot: copying them `ADDREF`s) from **owned temporaries** (`TMP_VAR`: the result of a call, `new`, `clone`, an array literal, a cast or operator producing a counted value; transferred by move, released by `FREE` when discarded, and released by `cleanup_live_vars` and `zend_vm_stack_free_args` when an exception abandons a frame or a half-built argument list).\n    62\t\n    63\tSubstrate: every activation (function body, and the global script) owns a **temp list** cell `php.rt/tmps` (a vector of counted values). The lowering classifies each expression syntactically:\n    64\t\n    65\t- **borrowed**: variable read, property read, dimension read, constant, literal scalar, static property read;\n    66\t- **owned**: call result, `new`, `clone`, array literal containing any non-literal element, `(array)`/`(object)` cast, `yield` result, closure creation, the result of an assignment expression used as a value (Zend copies it: borrowed-then-`ADDREF`), string interpolation (plain, uncounted), `match`/ternary results (owned iff the chosen arm is owned: the lowering registers at the arm).\n    67\t\n    68\tRules (one per handler):\n    69\t\n    70\t| # | Construct (handler) | Rule |\n    71\t|---|---|---|\n    72\t| 32 | owned temporary produced | `php/tmp! v`: if `v` is counted, push it on the activation's temp list and return it; uncounted values are not registered (the count is already held by the producing operation: a call result arrives with the callee's transferred count) |\n    73\t| 33 | `f(expr)` argument from a borrowed operand (`SEND_VAR`) | `ADDREF`, store into the callee's parameter slot |\n    74\t| 34 | argument from an owned temporary (`SEND_VAL`) | **move**: unregister from the caller's temp list, store into the parameter slot without `ADDREF` |\n    75\t| 35 | by-reference argument (`SEND_REF`) | row 5 or 4 into the parameter slot; a non-variable argument is `Notice: Only variables should be passed by reference` and a by-value send (the oracle pins which builtins are prefer-ref) |\n    76\t| 36 | `return expr` (`RETURN`) | borrowed → `ADDREF`; owned → unregister (move); the value becomes the **caller's** owned temporary (`php/tmp!` at the call site); then row 16 releases the callee's CVs and **every temporary still on the callee's list** |\n    77\t| 37 | expression statement, discarded call result (`FREE`) | at statement end `php/release-tmps mark`: release every temporary registered since the statement's mark, in registration order; a discarded `new Foo()` or a discarded call returning a fresh object is therefore destroyed, and its destructor runs, **before the next statement** |\n    78\t| 38 | temporary consumed by `STORE`/`ASSIGN` | owned → move (unregister, no `ADDREF`); borrowed → `ADDREF` |\n    79\t| 39 | exception or escape leaving an activation (`cleanup_live_vars`, `zend_vm_stack_free_args`) | the activation's `php/try-finally` runs `RELEASE_CVS` then releases the whole temp list; arguments already sent to a callee frame that never started are on the caller's temp list (sent-but-not-moved values stay registered until the call begins, at which point rows 33 and 34 apply), so they are released by the same finally |\n    80\t| 40 | `php/release-tmps` within a statement that spans a call | the mark is per statement; a temporary produced inside a nested expression and consumed by row 34 or 38 is gone before the mark is released; one that is neither consumed nor discarded (an owned operand of a comparison, a `??` left side) is released at the statement end |\n    81\t\n    82\tCost: one vector push per counted owned temporary and one release per statement that produced any; scalars, strings and plain arrays never touch the list.\n    83\t\n    84\tFixtures: php-src `Zend/tests/temporary_cleaning_0*.phpt` (the whole series, filtered to the supported surface), `tests/classes/destructor_*.phpt` where the destructor's timing is the assertion, and per row: discarded `new` with a destructor echoing; a call that throws after two arguments were evaluated, both objects with destructors (destroyed during unwinding, in order); `return $cv` versus `return new X`; a by-value `foreach` whose body reassigns the iterated variable.\n    85\t\n    86\t### A.4 Typed property references (P1-4): reference type sources\n    87\t\n    88\tA `zend_reference` bound to a typed property carries **type sources** (`zend_property_info` entries). Every assignment through the reference is verified against all sources (`zend_verify_ref_assignable_zval`), coercing in non-strict mode so that the result satisfies every source or raising `TypeError` (\"Cannot assign string to reference held by property C::$x of type int\"); sources are added when the reference is acquired on a typed property and removed when the property is unset, reassigned by `=&` to another reference, or the object is freed.\n    89\t\n    90\tSubstrate: the reference cell gains `:sources #{[o prop-name]}` (object handle and property name; the declared type is read from the class descriptor).\n    91\t\n    92\t| # | Construct | Rule |\n    93\t|---|---|---|\n    94\t| 41 | `$r =& $o->p` where `p` is typed (`ASSIGN_REF` on `FETCH_OBJ_W`) | `MAKE_REF` on the property slot; `sources += [o p]`; if the property is `readonly`: `Error: Cannot modify readonly property C::$p` unless acquired inside the declaring class scope on an uninitialized property (the oracle pins the 8.3 matrix); an uninitialized typed property vivifies to a reference holding the uninitialized sentinel, and a read through it before assignment is the \"must not be accessed before initialization\" `Error` |\n    95\t| 42 | `ASSIGN` through a reference with non-empty `:sources` | before mutation: for each source, verify `v` against the declared type under the **assigning unit's** `strict_types`; coercion (int→float, numeric-string→int/float, Stringable→string, int→string in coercive mode) is applied once and the coerced value must satisfy every source, else `TypeError` naming the first failing source; `null` requires a nullable type; then row 2 proceeds with the coerced value |\n    96\t| 43 | `$r++`, `$r .= …`, `$r += …` through a sourced reference (`PRE_INC`/`ASSIGN_OP` on `IS_REFERENCE`) | the result is verified as in row 42; an `int` property incremented past `PHP_INT_MAX` is `TypeError: Cannot increment property C::$p of type int past its maximal value` |\n    97\t| 44 | source removal | `unset($o->p)` → `sources −= [o p]` and the property becomes uninitialized (magic `__get` re-enabled for it); `$o->p =& other` → the old reference loses the source, the new gains it; object free (A.5 free step) → every property's reference loses `[o p]` |\n    98\t| 45 | two sources of different types on one reference (`$r =& $a->i; $b->s =& $r` with `int` and `string`) | the acquisition itself is checked: the current value must satisfy the new source, else `TypeError` at `=&`; thereafter row 42 requires both; a coercion that would satisfy one and not the other fails |\n    99\t| 46 | typed **static** properties | the same sources with `[class prop]` as the source key |\n   100\t| 47 | `list()`/`[]` destructuring by reference into typed properties (`[&$o->p] = $arr`) | row 41 per element, then row 42 at the assignment |\n   101\t\n   102\tFixtures: php-src `Zend/tests/type_declarations/typed_properties_*.phpt` restricted to those mentioning `&` or `reference` in `--FILE--` or `--TEST--`, and `typed_properties_0*` covering readonly and uninitialized states; plus the reviewer's program and one per row.\n   103\t\n   104\t### A.5 Object finalization and shutdown (P1-5): `zend_objects_store_del`, `zend_objects_destroy_object`, `zend_call_destructors`, `shutdown_executor`\n   105\t\n   106\tFinalization is not freeing. Zend: on reaching zero, if the destructor has not run, set `DESTRUCTOR_CALLED`, pin the object (`ADDREF`), run `__destruct` with the exception-chaining rule, unpin; if the count is still above zero the object was **resurrected** and storage is kept; otherwise the properties are destroyed and storage freed. A second drop to zero after resurrection frees without calling the destructor again. At request shutdown: shutdown functions, then destructors (globals that are objects with count 1, in reverse symbol-table order, repeated until stable; then every remaining object in the store in creation order), then output flush, then the symbol table is destroyed in reverse order, then remaining objects are freed with the destructed flag set.\n   107\t\n   108\tSubstrate: the object cell gains `:destructed? bool` and a creation ordinal `:ord`; the prelude keeps `php.rt/finalizable`, an **uncounted** ordered registry of objects whose class defines `__destruct` and which have not yet been destructed (uncounted: holding the cell ref there adds nothing to `:rc`; the entry is removed when the destructor runs). The exception-chaining rule is Zend's: if an exception is in flight when a destructor runs, it is set aside; a destructor exception then takes its place with the set-aside one as `previous`; otherwise the set-aside exception is restored.\n   109\t\n   110\t```text\n   111\tDESTROY(obj o)   [rc(o) reached 0]:\n   112\t  if not destructed?(o) and class defines __destruct:\n   113\t      destructed?(o) := true; rc(o) += 1 (pin); remove o from finalizable\n   114\t      run __destruct under the chaining rule; rc(o) -= 1 (unpin, exception-safe: the unpin is in a finally)\n   115\t      if rc(o) > 0 -> return                         (resurrected: storage kept, properties alive)\n   116\t  FREE(o): for each property slot: if a marker, drop [o p] from its sources; DELREF content; o is garbage\n   117\t```\n   118\t\n   119\t| # | Construct | Rule |\n   120\t|---|---|---|\n   121\t| 48 | last holder released | `DESTROY` above |\n   122\t| 49 | destructor resurrects `$this` (stores it in `$GLOBALS`, a static, or another live object) | `rc > 0` after unpin → kept; properties untouched; the destructor never runs again; the later final release goes straight to `FREE` |\n   123\t| 50 | destructor throws | chaining rule; the releasing operation (statement-end release, `unset`, scope exit) propagates the exception after completing its remaining releases (Zend releases the rest of the frame before the exception surfaces) |\n   124\t| 51 | `exit()` inside a destructor, destructor of the pending exception object | `exit` is the ordinary exit escape and proceeds to shutdown; destructing the exception currently in flight is `Fatal error: Attempt to destruct pending exception` (an `Error`-class halt in this VM, output text pinned) |\n   125\t| 22 (revised) | script end / request end | (1) run `register_shutdown_function` callbacks in registration order; (2) **destructor phase**: repeat { walk `$GLOBALS` in reverse insertion order; for each entry holding an object handle with `rc 1`, remove the entry and `DELREF` (destroying it) } until the entry count is stable; then call `__destruct` for every object remaining in `finalizable` in `:ord` order (pin/unpin as above, no freeing yet); scalars, strings and arrays in `$GLOBALS` are untouched here, so destructors can read them; (3) flush output buffers to the response stream; (4) release `$GLOBALS` in reverse insertion order, then static tables, then `FREE` every remaining object without calling destructors (flag set); an uncaught `Throwable` runs this sequence after the error output; the VM's operational halts (budget exhaustion, cancellation) skip the destructor phase, as Zend skips it on fatal errors |\n   126\t| 52 | `clone $o` (`zend_objects_clone_members`) | a fresh object cell (`rc 1`, `:ord` new, `destructed? false`); each property zval copied with `zval_add_ref` semantics: a marker whose reference has `rc 1` is **unwrapped** into the clone (value copied, `ADDREF`), any other counted content `ADDREF`ed (shared references stay shared, typed sources gain `[clone p]`); then `__clone` on the clone |\n   127\t\n   128\tFixtures: php-src `tests/classes/destructor_and_*.phpt`, `tests/classes/destructor_visibility_*`, `Zend/tests/objects_0*.phpt` (destructor and resurrection cases, filtered), `Zend/tests/bug*destruct*.phpt`, `Zend/tests/gc_*` only where no cycle collection is required (the others are corner C1 fixtures, expected to differ); per row: resurrection then final free (destructor once), destructor exception chaining with and without an in-flight exception, shutdown order with a destructor reading a global string created earlier and an object global with rc 1 versus rc 2, `exit` in a destructor, `clone` of an object with a shared and an unshared reference property.\n   129\t\n   130\t### A.6 Row renumbering and the earlier rows touched\n   131\t\n   132\tRow 9 is replaced by row 30; row 12 by rows 24 to 27; rows 1, 13 and 15 are refined by rows 32 to 40 (row 1 keeps \"copy\" for a borrowed source and \"move\" for an owned temporary); row 19 gains the typed-source hooks of A.4 and `clone` as row 52; row 20 is replaced by `DESTROY` of A.5; row 22 is revised above. The primitive procedures of round 5 stand with `DESTROY(obj)` replaced and `FETCH_UNSET` added.\n   133\t\n   134\t---\n   135\t\n   136\t## Part B. Convergence: the specified fragment and the known engine-fidelity corners\n   137\t\n   138\t### B.1 The support-profile statement\n   139\t\n   140\tThe `yang.php` runtime profile claims **exactly the rows of the transition table (rows 1 to 52 as revised) with their fixture sets**, verified against the pinned PHP 8.3 CLI on the three fixture sources (php-src `.phpt` selection, per-row programs, the permutation corpus). Every Zend behaviour outside those rows is listed below as a named corner with status `:restricted` (behaves differently, pinned by a departure fixture) or `:unsupported` (refused with a diagnostic), the deviation it implies, and the slice that would close it. The profile claims nothing about a corner beyond what its fixture pins. This is the project's standing pattern (C3's recorded departures, C2's finalization restriction, 8.5.6's refusal postures, the known-gaps ledger): an honest fragment with tracked gaps, not a claim of Zend fidelity. **None of the corners below is required by the Phase 3 exit criteria** (mixed text and output order; array ordering and alias/copy semantics; superglobals from explicit request input; type declarations not silently ignored, including through references per A.4; cross-request isolation), and none is required by the C4 import machinery; where a corner touched one of those, it was moved into the table in this round (typed-reference sources, destructor timing of discarded temporaries, unset without vivification).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
