tempt to destruct pending exception` | an `Error`-class halt with pinned text, not a process fatal | restricted | none planned |\n   155\t| C10 property hooks, asymmetric visibility | PHP 8.4 | not parsed (8.3 pinned) | unsupported | a grammar and version bump |\n   156\t| C11 dynamic scope references | `extract(EXTR_REFS)`, `compact` of references, `$$name =& …`, `get_defined_vars` | the dynamic-scope profile's symbol table holds markers like any location; `EXTR_REFS` and `$$x =&` are refused until P9 | unsupported until P9 | P9 |\n   157\t| C12 nested destructuring by reference | `[[&$a]] = …`, keyed nested forms | one level supported (row 47); deeper refused | restricted | P9 |\n   158\t| C13 `$GLOBALS` write semantics (8.1) | `$GLOBALS` is a read-only copy; element writes allowed; `$GLOBALS = …` compile error | element writes supported; whole-array operations on `$GLOBALS` refused | restricted | P9 |\n   159\t| C14 `ArrayObject`/`ArrayIterator` | SPL array wrappers sharing storage with an array by reference | deferred | unsupported | SPL slice |\n   160\t| C15 iterator repositioning on table rebuild | the exact `zend_hash_iterators_update` outcome for every rebuilding builtin | row 26 pins `unset`, append, assignment and `sort`; other rebuilding builtins (`array_splice`, `shuffle`, `array_unshift`) during by-ref iteration are pinned individually in P2 or listed here as restricted | restricted | P2 |\n   161\t| C16 `debug_zval_refcount`, `debug_zval_dump` counts | engine refcount numbers | refused (internal) | unsupported | none |\n   162\t| C17 destructor phase on engine fatals | fatal errors skip destructors; `E_ERROR` from the engine | this VM has no engine fatals; operational halts skip the phase (row 22) | restricted | none |\n   163\t| C18 `serialize`/`unserialize` of references, `__sleep`/`__wakeup`/`__serialize` | reference preservation `R:`/`r:` in serialized form | deferred | unsupported | serialization slice |\n   164\t| C19 `clone` of objects holding `Generator`/`Closure` state | engine-specific | pinned by oracle where supported; `Closure` clone supported, `Generator` clone is an `Error` as in the engine | restricted | P4-c |\n   165\t| C20 references to static properties across inheritance | redeclared static props and reference identity across child classes | row 46 covers the declaring class; inheritance edge cases pinned in P4 | restricted | P4 |\n   166\t| C21 memory and time limits | `memory_limit`, `max_execution_time`, `memory_get_usage` | the deterministic safepoint budget (B6) and no memory accounting; `memory_get_usage` returns a pinned constant | restricted | none (operational, §11) |\n   167\t| C22 `__destruct` output ordering with output buffers at shutdown | destructor `echo` inside an unflushed `ob_start` at shutdown | row 22 flushes after destructors; nested unclosed buffers are flushed in stack order; pinned | specified, listed for visibility | — |\n   168\t| C23 string interning and `===` | unobservable | none | — | — |\n   169\t\n   170\tEach corner's fixture is part of `ref-lifecycle-v1` (or `objects-v1`, `iteration-v1` for the new families) with its **expected departure recorded as data**, so the differential harness distinguishes a known corner from a row defect automatically; the corner table is the known-gaps ledger's PHP section, row for row, owner seat `yang.php`.\n   171\t\n   172\t### B.3 How the loop converges\n   173\t\n   174\tThe reviewer may now grant READY on the fragment: the table rows have per-row oracle fixtures, the three fixture sources are mechanical, and any Zend behaviour not in a row must be either in B.2 (tracked, with its deviation pinned) or a defect of the table (a fixture failure). A new finding therefore lands in one of three bins: a row defect (fix the row), a missing row that an exit criterion or the C4 profile needs (add the row, as this round did five times), or a corner (add to B.2 with its status and slice). Only the second bin blocks; the design states which bin by the criteria of B.1.\n   175\t\n   176\t---\n   177\t\n   178\t## Updated slices and owner decisions\n   179\t\n   180\t| Slice | Change |\n   181\t|---|---|\n   182\t| P2 | by-ref position table for every supported builtin (C6); rebuild-during-iteration pins (C15) |\n   183\t| P3 | rows 24 to 47 as listed; iterator cells on instances; temp lists; typed sources; the three new fixture families (`iteration-v1`, `objects-v1`, `temps-v1`) beside `ref-lifecycle-v1`; the php-src selection predicates extended to `foreachLoop.*`, `unset_*`, `temporary_cleaning_*`, `typed_properties_*` with references, `destructor_*`, `objects_0*` |\n   184\t| P4 | `DESTROY`/`FREE` states, resurrection, chaining, `clone` (row 52), shutdown sequence (row 22); typed-reference `TypeError` texts |\n   185\t| P4-b, P4-c | new follow-on slices named by the corners (object dimension containers; generator finalization) |\n   186\t\n   187\tOwner decisions: **B8.11 (new)** adopt the support-profile statement of B.1 and the corner table of B.2 as the PHP section of the known-gaps ledger (recommended); **B8.12 (new)** C1 cycle collection scheduled after the reclamation work rather than inside Phase 3 (recommended); **B8.8** stands (exact engine model for the specified fragment).\n   188\t\n   189\t## What round 6's reviewer should probe\n   190\t\n   191\t1. **Row 25's non-separating fetch**: the oracle fixture decides; if the pinned CLI prints 2 for the reviewer's program, the row flips and nothing else changes.\n   192\t2. **Row 26's repositioning rules** for `sort` and whole-array assignment through the loop reference, which I pinned by handler reading and the oracle must confirm.\n   193\t3. **The borrowed/owned classification** (A.3): whether any expression form yields an owned value the lowering treats as borrowed (candidates: `yield` results, `match` arms, string-to-array casts of literals) and whether an assignment expression's value is correctly treated as borrowed-then-`ADDREF`.\n   194\t4. **Row 42's coercion order with multiple sources** and the exact `TypeError` texts for sources of two classes.\n   195\t5. **Shutdown step 2's termination**: the repeat-until-stable walk and whether destructors that create new globals are handled as Zend does (they are re-walked; the oracle pins).\n   196\t6. **B.2's completeness**: whether any listed corner is in fact needed by the Phase 3 exit criteria or by the import machinery; the reviewer's candidate list should be checked against B.1's criterion, and anything that fails it moves into the table in the next round, which is the intended convergence path.\n2026-10-17 08:20:16 GMT\n2026-10-17 15:20:16 +0700\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
